t":"NYJ","home":true,"pf":48.0,"pa":20.0,"off_snaps":56,"epa_per_play":0.3274,"pass_rate":0.5714,"pass_rate_neutral":0.5263,"def_epa_per_play":-0.1568},{"week":16,"opponent":"DEN","home":false,"pf":34.0,"pa":20.0,"off_snaps":68,"epa_per_play":0.08766,"pass_rate":0.6029,"pass_rate_neutral":0.6667,"def_epa_per_play":-0.03301},{"week":17,"opponent":"IND","home":false,"pf":23.0,"pa":17.0,"off_snaps":68,"epa_per_play":0.005854,"pass_rate":0.5735,"pass_rate_neutral":0.6531,"def_epa_per_play":-0.2056},{"week":18,"opponent":"TEN","home":true,"pf":41.0,"pa":7.0,"off_snaps":55,"epa_per_play":0.2189,"pass_rate":0.6182,"pass_rate_neutral":0.8333,"def_epa_per_play":-0.3485}],"comps":{"offense":[{"season":2024,"team":"TB","distance":0.5037,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Liam Coen"},{"season":2022,"team":"NE","distance":0.5118,"shared":21,"play_caller":null},{"season":2024,"team":"HOU","distance":0.5734,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Bobby Slowik"},{"season":2025,"team":"DAL","distance":0.5858,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Brian Schottenheimer"},{"season":2025,"team":"MIN","distance":0.6023,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Kevin O'Connell"}],"defense":[{"season":2021,"team":"CIN","distance":0.5609,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Lou Anarumo"},{"season":2022,"team":"CIN","distance":0.6037,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Lou Anarumo"},{"season":2018,"team":"SEA","distance":0.6347,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2021,"team":"MIN","distance":0.6422,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2018,"team":"TB","distance":0.6446,"shared":18,"play_caller":null}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Jaguars went 13-4 in 2025 and outscored opponents 474 to 336. The defense did most of the work, finishing 3rd in EPA allowed while the offense ranked 14th in EPA per play. Their most distinctive trait was a Cover 6 rate that was among the highest in the league.","offense":"The Jaguars offense was a little above average by EPA per play. They threw more than almost anyone relative to the situation, even when the score did not call for it. Trevor Lawrence ran the passing game, with Parker Washington leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Jaguars had one of the best defenses in the league, ranking 6th or better in four of the five main efficiency measures. Its strongest area was takeaways per game, where it ranked 2nd, and its weakest was sack rate, where it ranked 29th. Travon Walker was as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise was Arik Armstead, who had 5.5 sacks despite playing only 60% of the snaps. In terms of scheme, they had a Cover 6 rate that was among the highest in the league.","special":"The Jaguars were about average in how often they went for it on fourth down. Their kickers made 88% of field goals, which was a little above average. Their salary cap was spread across positions in a fairly typical way. Their running backs were among the youngest in the league.","players":"Trevor Lawrence played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 4,007 yards. Targets were spread around, with Parker Washington and Brian Thomas Jr. leading a group where nobody took more than a fifth of the looks. Travis Etienne handled most of the work in the backfield with 66% of the carries. On defense, Josh Hines-Allen led the team with 8 sacks and Devin Lloyd picked off 5 passes.","coaching":"Liam Coen called the offense as head coach and Anthony Campanile called the defense. Their offense looked most like the 2024 Buccaneers under Liam Coen and the 2022 Patriots. Their defense looked most like the 2021 and 2022 Bengals, both under Lou Anarumo.","trends":"The offense went from 18th in EPA per play in 2024 to 14th in 2025, a modest improvement. The defense moved from 31st in EPA allowed per play in 2024 to 3rd in 2025, a big step forward. The biggest change in approach was Cover 6 rate, which went from 1% to 8%.","notes":"Every data source is available for 2025. This season uses the newer formation labels, which group singleback, I formation and empty sets together as under center, so those older formation rates are not shown."}}